success","api_error_status":null,"result":"I don't know which bank you mean, so check that fund's prospectus fee-table footnotes and SAI. The general structures are:\n\n**Unitary-fee arrangement**\n- The adviser charges one all-in fee and pays most fund expenses. Typical exclusions are brokerage, taxes, interest, acquired-fund fees and extraordinary costs.\n- A waiver usually just reduces that fee, often contractually through a stated date.\n- Such waivers are typically not recoupable. Since the adviser already bears expenses, the waived amount is forgone.\n\n**Ordinary expense-limitation agreement**\n- The adviser waives fees or reimburses expenses to keep total operating expenses under a cap.\n- Recoupment is commonly allowed within 36 months of the waiver.\n- It is usually limited to the lesser of the cap at the time of waiver and the current cap.\n- It often requires board approval, so future shareholders may bear past subsidies.\n\n**Bottom line:** unitary waivers are a permanent fee cut. Expense-limitation waivers are often a contingent deferral.","ttft_ms":885,"type":"result","duration_ms":3596,"uuid":"0eda78b0-daf2-4d6b-a884-0b8d1e1575d2","ttft_stream_ms":483,"time_to_request_ms":58,"first_content_frame_ms":483,"queued_turn_count":0,"result_index":0,"credential_expires_at":"2026-11-28T06:36:39.484Z"}
